"Wavelength (nm)",
"Ion",
"Fingerprints of the Elements | VirtualDemos",
"Fingerprints of the Elements",
"<strong>Modes in the demo:</strong> Explore (one element), Compare (two elements, one above the other) and Mystery (an unknown gas mixture or a star).",
"The picture shows the light source and a hand spectroscope, which spreads light out into its colors. The strip below it is what you would see in the eyepiece, from violet on the left to red on the right, with a scale in nanometers (nm).",
"Three kinds of source: <strong>Continuous</strong> is a hot lamp shining straight into the spectroscope; <strong>Emission</strong> is a tube of thin gas made to glow by electricity; <strong>Absorption</strong> is the lamp shining through a cell of cool gas.",
"Click an element in the periodic table to change the gas. Click or tap a line in the strip to read its wavelength; the readout also says which atom made it (“I” for a neutral atom, “II” for an atom that has lost one electron).",
"The lines are the strong lines measured by the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) for each element. Their wavelengths are given in air, as NIST and astronomers list them; in a vacuum each is about {#} % longer (hydrogen’s red line is {#} nm in air, {#} nm in vacuum). The widths and colors of the lines on the screen are drawn, not measured. In Absorption every listed line is drawn dark; a real cool gas shows fewer of them.",
"Record reading stores the source, the wavelength and the atom of the line you read last. Export to worksheet sends the readings to the Data table below; Clear starts a new series, which goes into a new copy of the table. In Mystery mode the source is stored as “Mystery”.",
"See the three kinds of spectra, and use the lines in a spectrum to find out which elements are in a gas or a star.",
"<strong>Spectra.</strong> A spectroscope spreads light out by wavelength, from violet (about {#} nm) to red (about {#} nm), the way a prism or a rainbow does. Around {#} Gustav Kirchhoff found that there are three kinds of spectra:",
"A hot, dense object (a glowing solid, liquid or dense gas, such as a lamp filament) gives a <strong>continuous spectrum</strong>: every color, with no gaps.",
"A hot, thin gas gives an <strong>emission spectrum</strong>: bright lines at a few wavelengths only, on a dark background.",
"A cool, thin gas in front of a hot, dense source gives an <strong>absorption spectrum</strong>: the continuous spectrum with dark lines where the gas has taken light out.",
"<strong>Fingerprints.</strong> The electrons in an atom can only have certain energies. When an electron drops from one energy level to a lower one, the atom gives off light of one exact wavelength; when it jumps up, it absorbs light of that same wavelength. Each element has its own set of energy levels, so each has its own pattern of lines, like a fingerprint. A star is a hot, dense ball with cooler, thinner gas at its surface, so a star shows an absorption spectrum, and its dark lines tell us what the star is made of.",
"Which of Kirchhoff’s three kinds of spectrum would you expect from: a light bulb; a neon sign; sunlight?",
"Hydrogen gas glowing in a tube gives a bright red line at {#} nm. If cool hydrogen gas sits in front of a bright lamp, where would you expect to see a dark line?",
"Why can astronomers learn what a star is made of without ever visiting it?",
"No hazards in the virtual lab. A real spectrum tube runs on several thousand volts: never touch one while it is on.",
"<strong>Part {#}. Kirchhoff’s three spectra.</strong> Choose hydrogen in Explore mode. Look at the strip with each of the three sources, Continuous, Emission and Absorption, and write down what you see for each (you will describe them in Analysis {#}).",
"<strong>Part {#}. Bright lines and dark lines.</strong> With hydrogen as an emission spectrum, record a reading of each of the four brightest visible lines (red, blue green, blue and violet). Then switch to absorption and record the same four lines, now dark. Send the readings to the worksheet, then clear them.",
"<strong>Part {#}. A fingerprint.</strong> Choose sodium as an emission spectrum and record a reading of each of its two brightest yellow lines. Then choose neon and record a reading of its brightest line. Send the readings to the worksheet, then clear them.",
"<strong>Part {#}. A mystery star.</strong> Choose Mystery mode and A star. Find four of the darkest lines in the star’s spectrum and record a reading of each, then send the readings to the worksheet. Now find which elements made them: click elements in the periodic table to add them to your guess, which appears in the bottom strip, until every dark line in the star matches a line of your guess. Check your answer, and write down which elements you found.",
"<span>B</span>Source",
